\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results presented in Section~\ref{sec:results} demonstrate that the
3.55 keV spectral anomaly detected within the Aether-Core/200LY
simulation environment is both statistically significant and robust
under a wide range of numerical and physical stress conditions. This
section discusses the implications of the anomaly, evaluates potential
sources of error, and outlines directions for further investigation.

\subsection{Physical Interpretation of the Anomaly}
The persistence of the 3.55 keV feature across multiple solver
configurations suggests that the anomaly is unlikely to originate from
numerical artifacts such as discretization error, floating-point drift,
or solver instability. Its spatial localization at
\[
(120, -45, 80) \; \text{LY}
\]
indicates that the anomaly is associated with a specific region of the
simulation domain rather than a global or boundary-driven effect.

The anomaly’s spectral shape is consistent with a narrow emission
feature, which may indicate:
\begin{itemize}
    \item localized plasma excitation,
    \item high-energy particle interactions,
    \item or an unresolved astrophysical structure.
\end{itemize}

Further physical modeling is required to determine whether the anomaly
corresponds to a known emission mechanism or represents a previously
uncharacterized phenomenon.

\subsection{Numerical Stability and Solver Reliability}
The anomaly remained stable under all stress-test conditions, including:
\begin{itemize}
    \item GPU memory pressure spikes,
    \item MPI jitter and node desynchronization,
    \item high-density N-body crowding,
    \item and floating-point perturbation sweeps.
\end{itemize}

These results indicate that the anomaly is not sensitive to transient
hardware conditions or solver perturbations. The strict satisfaction of
energy conservation,
\[
\frac{\Delta E}{E} < 10^{-6},
\]
and communication overhead constraints,
\[
\frac{T_{\text{comm}}}{T_{\text{total}}} < 0.12,
\]
further supports the reliability of the numerical environment.

\subsection{Surrogate Model Consistency}
The PINO surrogate model reproduced the anomaly with high confidence:
\[
C = 95.8\%.
\]
This suggests that the anomaly is encoded in the underlying physical
fields rather than arising from stochastic fluctuations or isolated
solver steps. The surrogate’s ability to predict the anomaly across
long-horizon rollouts reinforces the hypothesis that the feature is
physically meaningful.

\subsection{Potential Sources of Error}
Although the anomaly appears robust, several potential sources of error
must be considered:
\begin{itemize}
    \item inaccuracies in observational data preprocessing,
    \item systematic biases in density or magnetic field reconstruction,
    \item limitations in the ideal MHD model,
    \item or unmodeled physical processes.
\end{itemize}

Each of these factors warrants targeted investigation to rule out
non-physical origins.

\subsection{Implications for Future Work}
The detection of a stable, localized 3.55 keV anomaly raises several
avenues for future research:
\begin{itemize}
    \item independent reproduction using alternative multi-physics
          solvers,
    \item higher-resolution simulations of the anomaly region,
    \item incorporation of additional observational datasets,
    \item and exploration of non-ideal plasma effects.
\end{itemize}

If the anomaly persists under expanded modeling conditions, it may
represent a physically significant feature worthy of dedicated
astrophysical study.

\subsection{Summary}
The anomaly’s stability, reproducibility, and spatial coherence suggest
that it is not a numerical artifact. While further investigation is
required to determine its physical origin, the results indicate that the
Aether-Core/200LY engine provides a reliable platform for detecting and
characterizing emergent spectral features within complex astrophysical
environments.